theory_blocks.body:
Carbon dioxide dissolves in water to form a weak acid, carbonic acid. It is tempting to reason backwards from this and decide CO2 must therefore be 'the opposite', a base - but that confuses two separate questions.

The question that actually classifies an oxide is: [[blue:does it react with an acid, or with a base, to form a salt and water?]] CO2 reacts with sodium hydroxide (a base) to form sodium carbonate and water, which is the acidic-oxide pattern;

it does not react with an acid at all. Forming carbonic acid in water is consistent evidence for CO2 being acidic, not evidence against it.

